    {"text": "Docs: README documents the lock, how to turn it off, and the MCP server's refusal; module docstrings in firm.py, llm.py, graph.py and mcp_server.py state the behaviour. There is no CHANGELOG file in the repo, so no release-note line was written.", "proof_cmd": "ls CHANGELOG*; git show --stat b34945b 478e616 7cd4253 -- README.md", "proof_output": "ls: cannot access 'CHANGELOG*': No such file or directory; README.md changed in the T17 commits"}
  ],
  "files_changed": [],
  "dispositions": [
    {"finding": "verify-122 major: a saved ASGI app can bind a socket while the lock is on if the ASGI runner skips lifespan (src/cma/mcp_server.py:89)", "disposition": "not_reproduced", "evidence": "At b34945b sse_app() and streamable_http_app() raise NoASGIApp under lock on and off, so no app can be saved; with socket.socket.bind patched and the lock on, run('sse'), run('streamable-http') and run_sse_async refuse with binds=[]. tests: test_p18_no_asgi_app_is_handed_out_whatever_the_lock and test_p18_exported_mcp_server_refuses_to_start_under_lock pass."},
    {"finding": "verify-122 major: custom policy descriptions are copied verbatim into hosted prompts (src/cma/graph.py:174)", "disposition": "not_reproduced", "evidence": "rules_table(for_prompt=True) sends a description only if it equals the shipped policy's text for that id, and only R<digits> ids; the policy name is no longer sent. A CLI run with --policy carrying sentinels in a description, a rule id and the name gave 'leak False'. tests test_p5_a_custom_policys_free_text_never_reaches_the_prompt and ..._rule_id_or_description_... pass. RuleResult.detail is built from the metric's fixed name, method, value, op and threshold, and metric ids are validated against known metrics, so no other custom free text reaches the prompt."}
  ],
  "notes": "No code changed by this seat. Gaps for the owner, none needing code within scope: (1) The ContextVar that lets a run method build its app is inherited by tasks created while that run serves, so a tool handler inside an unlocked run could obtain an app; the lock was off at that start, so R29 is not touched, but it is a design-level leak of an app object. (2) A server started with the lock off keeps listening if the firm turns the lock on later; every tool call then refuses (tested), but the socket stays open until restart. Whether R29 means a running server must stop is a design question. (3) Calling the parent class unbound, MCPServer.sse_app(server), bypasses the subclass; that is deliberate circumvention outside the guarantee. (4) No remote alert exists, by design (Observability: local refusals only). The probe script lives in the session scratchpad, not in the repo or the run folder, so the runner did not run it; its claims are backed by the named pytest tests, which the runner does run."
}

